# Copy to `.env` beside compose.yaml and edit. `.env` is ignored by Git; this file is not, so nothing here is ever a
# secret. Every value below is a default the Compose file already applies, restated so the whole surface is visible in
# one place — an unset variable and the value shown here mean the same thing.
#
# Credentials do not live here. They are files under ./secrets/, mounted as Compose secrets, because a file can be
# permissioned and never appears in another process's environment block. Create these three before the first start:
#
#   mkdir -p secrets/mailmcp
#   chmod 700 secrets secrets/mailmcp
#   openssl rand -base64 33 | tr -d '\n' > secrets/postgres-superuser-password
#   openssl rand -base64 33 | tr -d '\n' > secrets/mailmcp-database-password
#   chmod 444 secrets/postgres-superuser-password secrets/mailmcp-database-password
#
# The directory restricts access and the files are readable, not the other way round: MailMcp runs as an unprivileged
# account that is nobody's host user, and Compose bind-mounts a secret with the host's own permissions rather than
# applying `mode`. A 0600 file reads to MailMcp as an unresolvable secret reference.

### The image
#
# Left at a name no registry can serve, so `docker compose up --build` builds this checkout and nothing is ever pulled
# by accident. There is no published MailMcp release yet: the Docker Hub release channel and the tags it carries are
# tracked by #156 and #116. Set this to a specific immutable tag once one exists — never to a moving tag, and never to
# a GHCR nightly, which is unsupported development output and needs the deliberate opt-in in compose.nightly.yaml.
# MAILMCP_IMAGE=mailmcp:local
# MAILMCP_MIGRATIONS_IMAGE=mailmcp-migrations:local
#
# `build` keeps Compose from ever pulling `mailmcp:local` from the configured registry, where an unrelated image
# published under that unqualified name would silently replace this checkout. Change it to `missing` in the same edit
# that points the two variables above at published release images — the image and how it is obtained are one decision.
# MAILMCP_PULL_POLICY=build

### Where MailMcp listens
#
# Loopback on purpose. MailMcp serves plain HTTP and terminates no TLS, so publishing this on an interface other than
# loopback exposes synchronized mail without transport protection. Change it only once a reverse proxy on the
# `frontend` network is what listens publicly.
# MAILMCP_HTTP_BIND=127.0.0.1
# MAILMCP_HTTP_PORT=8080

### The database
#
# The role MailMcp connects as owns its database and is not a superuser; the initialization script creates it and
# installs the `vector` extension while a superuser still can.
# MAILMCP_DATABASE=mailmcp
# MAILMCP_DATABASE_ROLE=mailmcp
# MAILMCP_POSTGRES_IMAGE=pgvector/pgvector:0.8.2-pg17
#
# The named volume holding the synchronized mail. `docker compose down` leaves it; only `down --volumes` destroys it,
# and rebuilding it costs a full IMAP resynchronization.
# MAILMCP_POSTGRES_VOLUME=mailmcp-postgres-data

### Bounds
#
# Logs are rotated so a long-running deployment cannot fill the disk with them.
# MAILMCP_LOG_MAX_SIZE=10m
# MAILMCP_LOG_MAX_FILES=3
#
# Resource limits. `deploy.resources.limits` is honored by `docker compose up` as well as by Swarm.
# MAILMCP_CPU_LIMIT=2
# MAILMCP_MEMORY_LIMIT=1g
# MAILMCP_POSTGRES_CPU_LIMIT=2
# MAILMCP_POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT=1g
